node_modules/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
.DS_Store

# Bootstrap-minted service tokens. Real credentials, 10-minute TTL, never committed.
compose/estate/tokens.env

# Rendered from a release manifest by scripts/release-render.py. Generated, never
# authored: committing it would let a stale overlay outlive the manifest it came from.
#
# THE GLOB, NOT THE ONE NAME. This rule named `docker-compose.release.yml` alone,
# and the moment a second environment appeared `release-render.py` wrote
# `docker-compose.release.testnet.yml` beside it — an identically generated file
# that the rule did not cover and that therefore showed up as untracked, inviting
# exactly the `git add` this comment argues against. One `.testnet` suffix should
# not be the difference between a generated artefact being ignored and being
# committed.
compose/docker-compose.release*.yml

# The operator's own environment for the estate's default project, read by compose
# from the repository root the way `compose/.env` is read from `compose/`.
#
# NOT A TEMPLATE AND NOT PLACEHOLDERS. On the live host this file holds a 28-char
# Grafana admin password and a 64-hex beacon token — real credentials for surfaces
# that are reachable from the network. It sat untracked-but-committable for the
# whole of the estate's life; the only reason it is not in the history is that
# nobody happened to run `git add -A` from the root.
#
# `.env.local` below already covers the convention this repository documents. This
# line covers the one the operator actually used, which is the point: an ignore
# file is only worth what it stops in practice.
/.env

# ── ANYTHING LOCAL, AND ESPECIALLY ANYTHING WITH A KEY IN IT ──────────────────
#
# The EMBER miner's wallet does NOT live here — it is at
# `~/.cloudsforge/ember-testnet/miner/coinbase-key.json`, outside every git work
# tree, so there is no ignore rule that can be edited away and no `git add -A`
# that can reach it. That is the primary defence and `estate-verify.sh` asserts
# it, along with the file's 0600 mode.
#
# This pattern is the second one, for the case that defence does not cover: a
# person following the estate's `.env.local` convention and putting an operator
# secret beside the compose file that reads it. `estate-verify.sh` checks this
# line with `git check-ignore -v` rather than assuming it, because an ignore rule
# that has never been tested is a rule nobody knows is wrong.
*.env.local
.env.local

# The gateway's local CA and leaf, minted by scripts/gateway-cert.sh.
#
# `ca.key` is a REAL certificate-authority private key. A CA the owner has
# installed in their login keychain will be trusted by every browser on the
# machine, so anyone holding this file could issue a certificate for any host
# that machine visits. It is generated locally, it never has to move, and there
# is no version of "convenient" that justifies committing it.
#
# The whole directory rather than `*.key`: `ca.srl` is the serial counter and a
# committed one silently reissues duplicate serials across clones, and the .crt
# files are derived — a committed certificate outliving the key that signed it is
# the stale-artefact failure this repository keeps finding.
#
# THIS DIRECTORY NOW ALSO HOLDS `origin.key`, and that raises the stakes rather
# than repeating them. It is the private key of a CLOUDFLARE ORIGIN CA
# certificate for `*.cloudsforge.online` and the bare apex, valid to 2041 —
# whoever holds it can terminate TLS for every hostname in the estate, for
# fifteen years, and there is no revocation path in this repository. It is placed
# by hand, is mode 0600, and no script here ever reads it. `trust.crt` is a
# derived bundle and lands here too, which is a third reason the rule is the
# directory and not a pattern.
gateway/certs/

# ── AND A PATTERN AS WELL AS THE DIRECTORY, DELIBERATELY BELT AND BRACES ──────
#
# The rule above is the one that matters and it is scoped to one path. This one
# costs nothing and covers the case that has actually happened in this
# repository twice, in other forms: material put NEXT TO where the rule looks.
# There is no private key anywhere in this tree that is meant to be committed, so
# a pattern with no exceptions is honest about the policy.
#
# `gateway/trust/*.pem` is unaffected and is committed on purpose: those are
# PUBLIC roots, and the extension difference is what keeps the two categories
# from ever being confused by an ignore rule.
*.key

# scripts/gateway-reload.sh's two working files, both DERIVED and both under the
# repository rather than /tmp for a reason it records at length: /tmp is not in
# the Lima VM's shared set, so a bind mount of it appears inside the container as
# an existing, empty, readable directory. The validator "passed" over nothing.
#
# `.gateway-validate/` is a copy of gateway/dynamic/ that a throwaway Traefik
# renders before the live one is touched; it is deleted on every exit path.
# `.gateway-loaded` is one line — the reload timestamp and the digest of the
# directory at that reload — and is a CACHE, never an authority: a missing or
# stale one falls through to "stale", so deleting it can only cause an
# unnecessary reload and never a missed one.
.gateway-validate/
.gateway-loaded
